  "body": "﻿## What changed\r\n\r\nThe picked Rotary Flow guide chains now read as ONE ordered set. The\r\nchain-set law lives in a new `rotary_flow_set` module of the motion\r\ncrate; the band loop of `rotary_flow` gains the shared-boundary dedup,\r\nthe single-chain lap, the sense normalization, and the set refusals.\r\n\r\n- **Multi-band.** Three or more ordered chains machine one band per\r\n  adjacent pair, and the SHARED boundary chain is cut once: band k > 0\r\n  starts at its first interior pass. Band k - 1's last pass already\r\n  walked the shared chain at the same stations, the same push-out and\r\n  the same climb setting, so nothing is lost.\r\n- **Single helical chain.** A lone chain machines the full revolution:\r\n  the kernel synthesizes the second boundary as the chain's own next\r\n  lap, one whole turn on in the unwrap's own theta (`th + TAU`), every\r\n  other coordinate held. The lap takes no whole-turn alignment (it is\r\n  exact by construction). The lap's two edges are the SAME world curve,\r\n  so the closing pass is dropped - the picked chain emits once.\r\n- **Sense normalization.** A chain wound against its pair is walked\r\n  backwards, said in the notes. This supersedes #2824's opposite-sense\r\n  refusal: the feature layer reads a chain's direction from the mesh,\r\n  never from the pick, so the refusal fired on legal picks the user\r\n  could not reverse (the probe on the bar showed the end-face rings\r\n  extracting wound -TAU while the profile rings extract +TAU). The raw\r\n  pairing of two oppositely wound chains twists the band a whole turn;\r\n  the reversal untwists it. Review rework (PR #4018 review, item 1):\r\n  the normalization now runs ONCE over the complete ordered set\r\n  before any band is cut (`normalize_set`), and the SAME normalized\r\n  guide bounds both adjacent bands - never a per-band copy, which\r\n  left the next band's lower boundary at the raw winding and flipped\r\n  the second band's cutting direction without a change to climb.\r\n- **Ring lap guard.** Review rework (item 2): a single guide that\r\n  turns about the axis at one axial station and one radius - a closed\r\n  ring or an open arc of one - is refused by name before a pass is\r\n  generated (`refuse_ring_lap`): its lap reads a positive angular\r\n  width but sweeps no band area, and the pass family would repeat one\r\n  closed cut. A guide that advances in station or in radius sweeps a\r\n  real band and passes.\r\n- **Refusals, all by name.** Chains that meet or cross are refused at\r\n  the shared spot (the exact segment-pair law, Ericson 5.1, at the\r\n  corner-lattice dust). Consecutive bands whose mean developed offsets\r\n  oppose (dot < 0) are refused as a set out of band order. A\r\n  perpendicular turn is not a doubling back: bands may tile around a\r\n  corner. Zero-length, non-finite and duplicate chains keep the #2824\r\n  refusals.\r\n\r\n## Geometry readings\r\n\r\n- The lap's developed width is exactly `R_ref * TAU` at the chain's own\r\n  outermost radius: `dth = TAU` and `dv = 0` at every matched place, so\r\n  `cross_width` reads one full revolution. The pass count divides it at\r\n  the stepover, and the emitted count is `ceil(width / step)` - the\r\n  closing pass is the picked chain itself.\r\n- The multi-band dedup emits `count_0 + count_k - (k)` passes over k\r\n  bands: band k > 0 takes `count - 1`, and the first interior pass sits\r\n  one stepover or less from the shared boundary, so the coverage never\r\n  opens a gap.\r\n- Refusal readings: the meet test is the exact segment-pair closest\r\n  distance (four endpoint candidates plus the clamped interior solve,\r\n  Ericson 5.1 as the guard cites it); the backtrack test compares the\r\n  mean developed offsets of consecutive pairs and refuses only a\r\n  strictly negative dot.\r\n\r\n## Pins\r\n\r\n- Motion, `tests/rotary_flow/bands.rs`: three ordered root helices cut\r\n  two bands in 7 passes (4 + 4 - 1) with pairwise-distinct landings;\r\n  one root helix covers the full revolution in 26 passes, the first\r\n  pass winding exactly `TAU`, gouge-free per the station pin; a ring\r\n  that crosses a corner chain refuses at the crossing's own spot.\r\n  Review rework pins: the middle pick reversed holds the direction\r\n  of every pass of every band for BOTH climb settings (aligned\r\n  control and mixed winding, pass by pass); a single closed ring\r\n  refuses the zero-area lap family by name; a single open ring arc\r\n  refuses the same.\r\n- Motion, unit: the lap morph, the meet law with its witness, the\r\n  backtrack law, the pass-count law, the backward walk (twice is the\r\n  identity, the knots stay ascending), the set normalization (mixed\r\n  winding resolved once, the cascade judged against the normalized\r\n  neighbour), the ring lap guard (a ring refuses, a helix and a\r\n  radial arc pass).\r\n- App, `tests/rotary_flow_band.rs`: three ring chains cut two bands,\r\n  the plunge count agrees with the bands' own stated counts, no two\r\n  passes land on one station, the notes state the shared boundary and\r\n  the sense walk.\r\n- App, `tests/rotary_flow_lap.rs` (new file; reason below): one\r\n  meridian chain machines the full revolution in 19 passes, one\r\n  landing per azimuth, lap note stated; the meridian-plus-ring set\r\n  refuses at the shared corner, with no path cached.\r\n\r\n## What was not exercised\r\n\r\n- The app-level pins run on the turned bar, not a helical screw: the\r\n  app's slicing refuses open meshes, and a closed screw fixture with\r\n  sharp helical edges is its own fixture work. The helical single-chain\r\n  and multi-band coverage is pinned at the motion level on the groove\r\n  fixture (one-turn and three-turn rotors, station and swept-ball\r\n  proofs).\r\n- The heavy lanes are not run (#3556: no PR gets the full heavy lanes;\r\n  this is a P7 under the safety floor, so no targeted heavy step is\r\n  owed). The light lane ran partially green (motion light: lib, the\r\n  adaptive troch twins, app light: pocket_pass_start); its\r\n  all-binaries release build step failed on machine disk during the\r\n  run, not on code - the same build passed clean after a full rebuild\r\n  of the touched crates.\r\n- No example catalog case exercises Rotary Flow, so their outputs\r\n  cannot change; the quick plan still ran all three catalog suites,\r\n  green.\r\n- The quick gate's app prebuild wedged once (#3675) and the app suites\r\n  were run one by one instead: all 20 planned suites green, clippy\r\n  `-D warnings` clean on both touched crates.\r\n- Review rework head (99283bc76, after the merge of main 49bfcaea8):\r\n  the rework gate ran `cargo test -p camcraft-motion --lib` (683\r\n  passed, 0 failed, 22 ignored), `cargo test -p camcraft-motion\r\n  --test rotary_flow` (14 passed, 1 heavy-ignored), `cargo test -p\r\n  camcraft-app --test rotary_flow_band` (7 passed), `cargo test -p\r\n  camcraft-app --test rotary_flow_lap` (2 passed), the three new\r\n  tests by name, and clippy `-D warnings` clean on\r\n  `camcraft-motion --all-targets` and on the touched\r\n  `camcraft-app --test rotary_flow_band` binary. The light lane of\r\n  the first head stands; the rework diff touches only the flow\r\n  kernel and its own pins, and the review re-runs what it needs.\r\n- The crossing between NON-adjacent guides that the review named\r\n  non-blocking (pre-existing on main) is filed as #4032 and stays\r\n  out of this branch.\r\n\r\n## File note (#3187)\r\n\r\n`crates/app/tests/rotary_flow_lap.rs` is a new top-level test file: the\r\nrotary domain keeps one top-level file per topic, and a second file\r\nholds these laws so `rotary_flow_band.rs` stays under the 400-line\r\nceiling.\r\n\r\n## Changelog\r\n\r\nRotary Flow: three or more ordered guide chains machine one band per\r\nadjacent pair with the shared boundary cut once; a single guide chain\r\nmachines the full revolution as its own next lap; the complete set is\r\nwound one way before the bands are cut, so a reversed pick never flips\r\na band's cutting direction; a single ring guide - closed or an open\r\narc - is refused by name, its lap sweeps no band area; chains that\r\nmeet or cross, and sets out of band order, are refused by name.\r\n\r\nFixes #2829\r\n\r\n",
